# E-Commerce Data Warehouse Project Structure Rules

## Project Organization
- MUST follow the documented project structure in README.md
- MUST organize files into: data/, faker/, spark/, airflow/, sql/
- MUST maintain separation between raw, cleaned, and curated data directories
- MUST keep DAG files in airflow/dags/ directory

## Directory Requirements
- data/ directory MUST contain: raw/, cleaned/, curated/ subdirectories
- faker/ directory MUST contain: generate_customers.py, generate_products.py, generate_orders.py, generate_payments.py
- spark/ directory MUST contain: clean_customers.py, transform_orders.py, build_fact_orders.py, build_dimensions.py
- airflow/dags/ directory MUST contain: ecommerce_etl_dag.py
- sql/ directory MUST contain: analytics_queries.sql

## File Naming Conventions
- Python files MUST use snake_case (e.g., generate_customers.py)
- SQL files MUST use snake_case (e.g., analytics_queries.sql)
- Configuration files MUST use .conf or .ini extension
- Data files MUST use appropriate extensions (CSV, JSON, Parquet)

## Code Organization
- Each module MUST have a clear, single responsibility
- Data generation scripts MUST be separate from transformation scripts
- Airflow DAGs MUST be modular and reusable
- SQL queries MUST be organized by purpose (analytics, reporting, etc.)

## Documentation Requirements
- Each Python module MUST include docstrings
- Each DAG MUST document dependencies and schedule
- SQL queries MUST include comments explaining business logic
- README.md MUST be kept up-to-date with project changes

## Version Control
- Data files SHOULD NOT be committed to version control (use .gitignore)
- Configuration files MUST be environment-specific
- Schema changes MUST be documented
- Pipeline changes MUST include migration notes